            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-56722",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: RDMA/hns: Fix cpu stuck caused by printings during reset"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/hns: Fix cpu stuck caused by printings during reset\n\nDuring reset, cmd to destroy resources such as qp, cq, and mr may fail,\nand error logs will be printed. When a large number of resources are\ndestroyed, there will be lots of printings, and it may lead to a cpu\nstuck.\n\nDelete some unnecessary printings and replace other printing functions\nin these paths with the ratelimited version."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56722",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-56722\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-58.60\nLink: [CVE-2024-56722](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56722)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/hns: Fix cpu stuck caused by printings during reset\n\nDuring reset, cmd to destroy resources such as qp, cq, and mr may fail,\nand error logs will be printed. When a large number of resources are\ndestroyed, there will be lots of printings, and it may lead to a cpu\nstuck.\n\nDelete some unnecessary printings and replace other printing functions\nin these paths with the ratelimited version.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-56722**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-58.60|[CVE-2024-56722](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56722)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/hns: Fix cpu stuck caused by printings during reset\n\nDuring reset, cmd to destroy resources such as qp, cq, and mr may fail,\nand error logs will be printed. When a large number of resources are\ndestroyed, there will be lots of printings, and it may lead to a cpu\nstuck.\n\nDelete some unnecessary printings and replace other printing functions\nin these paths with the ratelimited version."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-56723",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: mfd: intel_soc_pmic_bxtwc: Use IRQ domain for PMIC devices"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmfd: intel_soc_pmic_bxtwc: Use IRQ domain for PMIC devices\n\nWhile design wise the idea of converting the driver to use\nthe hierarchy of the IRQ chips is correct, the implementation\nhas (inherited) flaws. This was unveiled when platform_get_irq()\nhad started WARN() on IRQ 0 that is supposed to be a Linux\nIRQ number (also known as vIRQ).\n\nRework the driver to respect IRQ domain when creating each MFD\ndevice separately, as the domain is not the same for all of them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56723",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-56723\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-58.60\nLink: [CVE-2024-56723](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56723)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmfd: intel_soc_pmic_bxtwc: Use IRQ domain for PMIC devices\n\nWhile design wise the idea of converting the driver to use\nthe hierarchy of the IRQ chips is correct, the implementation\nhas (inherited) flaws. This was unveiled when platform_get_irq()\nhad started WARN() on IRQ 0 that is supposed to be a Linux\nIRQ number (also known as vIRQ).\n\nRework the driver to respect IRQ domain when creating each MFD\ndevice separately, as the domain is not the same for all of them.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-56723**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-58.60|[CVE-2024-56723](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-56723)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmfd: intel_soc_pmic_bxtwc: Use IRQ domain for PMIC devices\n\nWhile design wise the idea of converting the driver to use\nthe hierarchy of the IRQ chips is correct, the implementation\nhas (inherited) flaws. This was unveiled when platform_get_irq()\nhad started WARN() on IRQ 0 that is supposed to be a Linux\nIRQ number (also known as vIRQ).\n\nRework the driver to respect IRQ domain when creating each MFD\ndevice separately, as the domain is not the same for all of them."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
