            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-57910",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: iio: light: vcnl4035: fix information leak in triggered buffer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: light: vcnl4035: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'buffer' local array is used to push data to userspace from a\ntriggered buffer, but it does not set an initial value for the single\ndata element, which is an u16 aligned to 8 bytes. That leaves at least\n4 bytes uninitialized even after writing an integer value with\nregmap_read().\n\nInitialize the array to zero before using it to avoid pushing\nuninitialized information to userspace."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57910",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-57910\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-60.63\nLink: [CVE-2024-57910](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57910)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: light: vcnl4035: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'buffer' local array is used to push data to userspace from a\ntriggered buffer, but it does not set an initial value for the single\ndata element, which is an u16 aligned to 8 bytes. That leaves at least\n4 bytes uninitialized even after writing an integer value with\nregmap_read().\n\nInitialize the array to zero before using it to avoid pushing\nuninitialized information to userspace.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-57910**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-60.63|[CVE-2024-57910](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57910)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: light: vcnl4035: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'buffer' local array is used to push data to userspace from a\ntriggered buffer, but it does not set an initial value for the single\ndata element, which is an u16 aligned to 8 bytes. That leaves at least\n4 bytes uninitialized even after writing an integer value with\nregmap_read().\n\nInitialize the array to zero before using it to avoid pushing\nuninitialized information to userspace."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-57911",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: iio: dummy: iio_simply_dummy_buffer: fix information leak in triggered buffer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: dummy: iio_simply_dummy_buffer: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'data' array is allocated via kmalloc() and it is used to push data\nto user space from a triggered buffer, but it does not set values for\ninactive channels, as it only uses iio_for_each_active_channel()\nto assign new values.\n\nUse kzalloc for the memory allocation to avoid pushing uninitialized\ninformation to userspace."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57911",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-57911\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-60.63\nLink: [CVE-2024-57911](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57911)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: dummy: iio_simply_dummy_buffer: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'data' array is allocated via kmalloc() and it is used to push data\nto user space from a triggered buffer, but it does not set values for\ninactive channels, as it only uses iio_for_each_active_channel()\nto assign new values.\n\nUse kzalloc for the memory allocation to avoid pushing uninitialized\ninformation to userspace.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-57911**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-60.63|[CVE-2024-57911](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-57911)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\niio: dummy: iio_simply_dummy_buffer: fix information leak in triggered buffer\n\nThe 'data' array is allocated via kmalloc() and it is used to push data\nto user space from a triggered buffer, but it does not set values for\ninactive channels, as it only uses iio_for_each_active_channel()\nto assign new values.\n\nUse kzalloc for the memory allocation to avoid pushing uninitialized\ninformation to userspace."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
