            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-58072",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: rtlwifi: remove unused check_buddy_priv"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: rtlwifi: remove unused check_buddy_priv\n\nCommit 2461c7d60f9f (\"rtlwifi: Update header file\") introduced a global\nlist of private data structures.\n\nLater on, commit 26634c4b1868 (\"rtlwifi Modify existing bits to match\nvendor version 2013.02.07\") started adding the private data to that list at\nprobe time and added a hook, check_buddy_priv to find the private data from\na similar device.\n\nHowever, that function was never used.\n\nBesides, though there is a lock for that list, it is never used. And when\nthe probe fails, the private data is never removed from the list. This\nwould cause a second probe to access freed memory.\n\nRemove the unused hook, structures and members, which will prevent the\npotential race condition on the list and its corruption during a second\nprobe when probe fails."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58072",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-58072\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-64.67\nLink: [CVE-2024-58072](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58072)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: rtlwifi: remove unused check_buddy_priv\n\nCommit 2461c7d60f9f (\"rtlwifi: Update header file\") introduced a global\nlist of private data structures.\n\nLater on, commit 26634c4b1868 (\"rtlwifi Modify existing bits to match\nvendor version 2013.02.07\") started adding the private data to that list at\nprobe time and added a hook, check_buddy_priv to find the private data from\na similar device.\n\nHowever, that function was never used.\n\nBesides, though there is a lock for that list, it is never used. And when\nthe probe fails, the private data is never removed from the list. This\nwould cause a second probe to access freed memory.\n\nRemove the unused hook, structures and members, which will prevent the\npotential race condition on the list and its corruption during a second\nprobe when probe fails.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-58072**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-64.67|[CVE-2024-58072](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58072)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: rtlwifi: remove unused check_buddy_priv\n\nCommit 2461c7d60f9f (\"rtlwifi: Update header file\") introduced a global\nlist of private data structures.\n\nLater on, commit 26634c4b1868 (\"rtlwifi Modify existing bits to match\nvendor version 2013.02.07\") started adding the private data to that list at\nprobe time and added a hook, check_buddy_priv to find the private data from\na similar device.\n\nHowever, that function was never used.\n\nBesides, though there is a lock for that list, it is never used. And when\nthe probe fails, the private data is never removed from the list. This\nwould cause a second probe to access freed memory.\n\nRemove the unused hook, structures and members, which will prevent the\npotential race condition on the list and its corruption during a second\nprobe when probe fails."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-58076",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: clk: qcom: gcc-sm6350: Add missing parent_map for two clocks"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nclk: qcom: gcc-sm6350: Add missing parent_map for two clocks\n\nIf a clk_rcg2 has a parent, it should also have parent_map defined,\notherwise we'll get a NULL pointer dereference when calling clk_set_rate\nlike the following:\n\n  [    3.388105] Call trace:\n  [    3.390664]  qcom_find_src_index+0x3c/0x70 (P)\n  [    3.395301]  qcom_find_src_index+0x1c/0x70 (L)\n  [    3.399934]  _freq_tbl_determine_rate+0x48/0x100\n  [    3.404753]  clk_rcg2_determine_rate+0x1c/0x28\n  [    3.409387]  clk_core_determine_round_nolock+0x58/0xe4\n  [    3.421414]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0x48/0xfc\n  [    3.432974]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0xd0/0xfc\n  [    3.444483]  clk_core_set_rate_nolock+0x8c/0x300\n  [    3.455886]  clk_set_rate+0x38/0x14c\n\nAdd the parent_map property for two clocks where it's missing and also\nun-inline the parent_data as well to keep the matching parent_map and\nparent_data together."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58076",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-58076\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-64.67\nLink: [CVE-2024-58076](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58076)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nclk: qcom: gcc-sm6350: Add missing parent_map for two clocks\n\nIf a clk_rcg2 has a parent, it should also have parent_map defined,\notherwise we'll get a NULL pointer dereference when calling clk_set_rate\nlike the following:\n\n  [    3.388105] Call trace:\n  [    3.390664]  qcom_find_src_index+0x3c/0x70 (P)\n  [    3.395301]  qcom_find_src_index+0x1c/0x70 (L)\n  [    3.399934]  _freq_tbl_determine_rate+0x48/0x100\n  [    3.404753]  clk_rcg2_determine_rate+0x1c/0x28\n  [    3.409387]  clk_core_determine_round_nolock+0x58/0xe4\n  [    3.421414]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0x48/0xfc\n  [    3.432974]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0xd0/0xfc\n  [    3.444483]  clk_core_set_rate_nolock+0x8c/0x300\n  [    3.455886]  clk_set_rate+0x38/0x14c\n\nAdd the parent_map property for two clocks where it's missing and also\nun-inline the parent_data as well to keep the matching parent_map and\nparent_data together.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-58076**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-64.67|[CVE-2024-58076](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58076)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nclk: qcom: gcc-sm6350: Add missing parent_map for two clocks\n\nIf a clk_rcg2 has a parent, it should also have parent_map defined,\notherwise we'll get a NULL pointer dereference when calling clk_set_rate\nlike the following:\n\n  [    3.388105] Call trace:\n  [    3.390664]  qcom_find_src_index+0x3c/0x70 (P)\n  [    3.395301]  qcom_find_src_index+0x1c/0x70 (L)\n  [    3.399934]  _freq_tbl_determine_rate+0x48/0x100\n  [    3.404753]  clk_rcg2_determine_rate+0x1c/0x28\n  [    3.409387]  clk_core_determine_round_nolock+0x58/0xe4\n  [    3.421414]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0x48/0xfc\n  [    3.432974]  clk_core_round_rate_nolock+0xd0/0xfc\n  [    3.444483]  clk_core_set_rate_nolock+0x8c/0x300\n  [    3.455886]  clk_set_rate+0x38/0x14c\n\nAdd the parent_map property for two clocks where it's missing and also\nun-inline the parent_data as well to keep the matching parent_map and\nparent_data together."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-58077",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ASoC: soc-pcm: don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: soc-pcm: don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback\n\ncommit 1f5664351410 (\"ASoC: lower \"no backend DAIs enabled for ... Port\"\nlog severity\") ignores -EINVAL error message on common soc_pcm_ret().\nIt is used from many functions, ignoring -EINVAL is over-kill.\n\nThe reason why -EINVAL was ignored was it really should only be used\nupon invalid parameters coming from userspace and in that case we don't\nwant to log an error since we do not want to give userspace a way to do\na denial-of-service attack on the syslog / diskspace.\n\nSo don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback is better idea."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58077",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2024-58077\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-64.67\nLink: [CVE-2024-58077](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58077)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: soc-pcm: don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback\n\ncommit 1f5664351410 (\"ASoC: lower \"no backend DAIs enabled for ... Port\"\nlog severity\") ignores -EINVAL error message on common soc_pcm_ret().\nIt is used from many functions, ignoring -EINVAL is over-kill.\n\nThe reason why -EINVAL was ignored was it really should only be used\nupon invalid parameters coming from userspace and in that case we don't\nwant to log an error since we do not want to give userspace a way to do\na denial-of-service attack on the syslog / diskspace.\n\nSo don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback is better idea.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2024-58077**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-64.67|[CVE-2024-58077](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2024-58077)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: soc-pcm: don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback\n\ncommit 1f5664351410 (\"ASoC: lower \"no backend DAIs enabled for ... Port\"\nlog severity\") ignores -EINVAL error message on common soc_pcm_ret().\nIt is used from many functions, ignoring -EINVAL is over-kill.\n\nThe reason why -EINVAL was ignored was it really should only be used\nupon invalid parameters coming from userspace and in that case we don't\nwant to log an error since we do not want to give userspace a way to do\na denial-of-service attack on the syslog / diskspace.\n\nSo don't use soc_pcm_ret() on .prepare callback is better idea."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
