            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-21779",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: KVM: x86: Reject Hyper-V's SEND_IPI hypercalls if local APIC isn't in-kernel"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: x86: Reject Hyper-V's SEND_IPI hypercalls if local APIC isn't in-kernel\n\nAdvertise support for Hyper-V's SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX hypercalls if and\nonly if the local API is emulated/virtualized by KVM, and explicitly reject\nsaid hypercalls if the local APIC is emulated in userspace, i.e. don't rely\non userspace to opt-in to KVM_CAP_HYPERV_ENFORCE_CPUID.\n\nRejecting SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX fixes a NULL-pointer dereference if\nHyper-V enlightenments are exposed to the guest without an in-kernel local\nAPIC:\n\n  dump_stack+0xbe/0xfd\n  __kasan_report.cold+0x34/0x84\n  kasan_report+0x3a/0x50\n  __apic_accept_irq+0x3a/0x5c0\n  kvm_hv_send_ipi.isra.0+0x34e/0x820\n  kvm_hv_hypercall+0x8d9/0x9d0\n  kvm_emulate_hypercall+0x506/0x7e0\n  __vmx_handle_exit+0x283/0xb60\n  vmx_handle_exit+0x1d/0xd0\n  vcpu_enter_guest+0x16b0/0x24c0\n  vcpu_run+0xc0/0x550\n  kvm_arch_vcpu_ioctl_run+0x170/0x6d0\n  kvm_vcpu_ioctl+0x413/0xb20\n  __se_sys_ioctl+0x111/0x160\n  do_syscal1_64+0x30/0x40\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x67/0xd1\n\nNote, checking the sending vCPU is sufficient, as the per-VM irqchip_mode\ncan't be modified after vCPUs are created, i.e. if one vCPU has an\nin-kernel local APIC, then all vCPUs have an in-kernel local APIC."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21779",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-21779\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-78.78\nLink: [CVE-2025-21779](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21779)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: x86: Reject Hyper-V's SEND_IPI hypercalls if local APIC isn't in-kernel\n\nAdvertise support for Hyper-V's SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX hypercalls if and\nonly if the local API is emulated/virtualized by KVM, and explicitly reject\nsaid hypercalls if the local APIC is emulated in userspace, i.e. don't rely\non userspace to opt-in to KVM_CAP_HYPERV_ENFORCE_CPUID.\n\nRejecting SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX fixes a NULL-pointer dereference if\nHyper-V enlightenments are exposed to the guest without an in-kernel local\nAPIC:\n\n  dump_stack+0xbe/0xfd\n  __kasan_report.cold+0x34/0x84\n  kasan_report+0x3a/0x50\n  __apic_accept_irq+0x3a/0x5c0\n  kvm_hv_send_ipi.isra.0+0x34e/0x820\n  kvm_hv_hypercall+0x8d9/0x9d0\n  kvm_emulate_hypercall+0x506/0x7e0\n  __vmx_handle_exit+0x283/0xb60\n  vmx_handle_exit+0x1d/0xd0\n  vcpu_enter_guest+0x16b0/0x24c0\n  vcpu_run+0xc0/0x550\n  kvm_arch_vcpu_ioctl_run+0x170/0x6d0\n  kvm_vcpu_ioctl+0x413/0xb20\n  __se_sys_ioctl+0x111/0x160\n  do_syscal1_64+0x30/0x40\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x67/0xd1\n\nNote, checking the sending vCPU is sufficient, as the per-VM irqchip_mode\ncan't be modified after vCPUs are created, i.e. if one vCPU has an\nin-kernel local APIC, then all vCPUs have an in-kernel local APIC.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-21779**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-78.78|[CVE-2025-21779](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21779)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: x86: Reject Hyper-V's SEND_IPI hypercalls if local APIC isn't in-kernel\n\nAdvertise support for Hyper-V's SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX hypercalls if and\nonly if the local API is emulated/virtualized by KVM, and explicitly reject\nsaid hypercalls if the local APIC is emulated in userspace, i.e. don't rely\non userspace to opt-in to KVM_CAP_HYPERV_ENFORCE_CPUID.\n\nRejecting SEND_IPI and SEND_IPI_EX fixes a NULL-pointer dereference if\nHyper-V enlightenments are exposed to the guest without an in-kernel local\nAPIC:\n\n  dump_stack+0xbe/0xfd\n  __kasan_report.cold+0x34/0x84\n  kasan_report+0x3a/0x50\n  __apic_accept_irq+0x3a/0x5c0\n  kvm_hv_send_ipi.isra.0+0x34e/0x820\n  kvm_hv_hypercall+0x8d9/0x9d0\n  kvm_emulate_hypercall+0x506/0x7e0\n  __vmx_handle_exit+0x283/0xb60\n  vmx_handle_exit+0x1d/0xd0\n  vcpu_enter_guest+0x16b0/0x24c0\n  vcpu_run+0xc0/0x550\n  kvm_arch_vcpu_ioctl_run+0x170/0x6d0\n  kvm_vcpu_ioctl+0x413/0xb20\n  __se_sys_ioctl+0x111/0x160\n  do_syscal1_64+0x30/0x40\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x67/0xd1\n\nNote, checking the sending vCPU is sufficient, as the per-VM irqchip_mode\ncan't be modified after vCPUs are created, i.e. if one vCPU has an\nin-kernel local APIC, then all vCPUs have an in-kernel local APIC."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-21781",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: batman-adv: fix panic during interface removal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbatman-adv: fix panic during interface removal\n\nReference counting is used to ensure that\nbatadv_hardif_neigh_node and batadv_hard_iface\nare not freed before/during\nbatadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update work is\nfinished.\n\nBut there isn't a guarantee that the hard if will\nremain associated with a soft interface up until\nthe work is finished.\n\nThis fixes a crash triggered by reboot that looks\nlike this:\n\nCall trace:\n batadv_v_mesh_free+0xd0/0x4dc [batman_adv]\n batadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update+0x1c/0xa4\n process_one_work+0x178/0x398\n worker_thread+0x2e8/0x4d0\n kthread+0xd8/0xdc\n ret_from_fork+0x10/0x20\n\n(the batadv_v_mesh_free call is misleading,\nand does not actually happen)\n\nI was able to make the issue happen more reliably\nby changing hardif_neigh-\u003ebat_v.metric_work work\nto be delayed work. This allowed me to track down\nand confirm the fix.\n\n[sven@narfation.org: prevent entering batadv_v_elp_get_throughput without\n soft_iface]"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21781",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-21781\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-78.78\nLink: [CVE-2025-21781](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21781)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbatman-adv: fix panic during interface removal\n\nReference counting is used to ensure that\nbatadv_hardif_neigh_node and batadv_hard_iface\nare not freed before/during\nbatadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update work is\nfinished.\n\nBut there isn't a guarantee that the hard if will\nremain associated with a soft interface up until\nthe work is finished.\n\nThis fixes a crash triggered by reboot that looks\nlike this:\n\nCall trace:\n batadv_v_mesh_free+0xd0/0x4dc [batman_adv]\n batadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update+0x1c/0xa4\n process_one_work+0x178/0x398\n worker_thread+0x2e8/0x4d0\n kthread+0xd8/0xdc\n ret_from_fork+0x10/0x20\n\n(the batadv_v_mesh_free call is misleading,\nand does not actually happen)\n\nI was able to make the issue happen more reliably\nby changing hardif_neigh-\u003ebat_v.metric_work work\nto be delayed work. This allowed me to track down\nand confirm the fix.\n\n[sven@narfation.org: prevent entering batadv_v_elp_get_throughput without\n soft_iface]",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-21781**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-78.78|[CVE-2025-21781](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21781)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbatman-adv: fix panic during interface removal\n\nReference counting is used to ensure that\nbatadv_hardif_neigh_node and batadv_hard_iface\nare not freed before/during\nbatadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update work is\nfinished.\n\nBut there isn't a guarantee that the hard if will\nremain associated with a soft interface up until\nthe work is finished.\n\nThis fixes a crash triggered by reboot that looks\nlike this:\n\nCall trace:\n batadv_v_mesh_free+0xd0/0x4dc [batman_adv]\n batadv_v_elp_throughput_metric_update+0x1c/0xa4\n process_one_work+0x178/0x398\n worker_thread+0x2e8/0x4d0\n kthread+0xd8/0xdc\n ret_from_fork+0x10/0x20\n\n(the batadv_v_mesh_free call is misleading,\nand does not actually happen)\n\nI was able to make the issue happen more reliably\nby changing hardif_neigh-\u003ebat_v.metric_work work\nto be delayed work. This allowed me to track down\nand confirm the fix.\n\n[sven@narfation.org: prevent entering batadv_v_elp_get_throughput without\n soft_iface]"
              },
