            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-21898",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ftrace: Avoid potential division by zero in function_stat_show()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nftrace: Avoid potential division by zero in function_stat_show()\n\nCheck whether denominator expression x * (x - 1) * 1000 mod {2^32, 2^64}\nproduce zero and skip stddev computation in that case.\n\nFor now don't care about rec-\u003ecounter * rec-\u003ecounter overflow because\nrec-\u003etime * rec-\u003etime overflow will likely happen earlier."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21898",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-21898\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-84.84\nLink: [CVE-2025-21898](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21898)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nftrace: Avoid potential division by zero in function_stat_show()\n\nCheck whether denominator expression x * (x - 1) * 1000 mod {2^32, 2^64}\nproduce zero and skip stddev computation in that case.\n\nFor now don't care about rec-\u003ecounter * rec-\u003ecounter overflow because\nrec-\u003etime * rec-\u003etime overflow will likely happen earlier.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-21898**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-84.84|[CVE-2025-21898](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-21898)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nftrace: Avoid potential division by zero in function_stat_show()\n\nCheck whether denominator expression x * (x - 1) * 1000 mod {2^32, 2^64}\nproduce zero and skip stddev computation in that case.\n\nFor now don't care about rec-\u003ecounter * rec-\u003ecounter overflow because\nrec-\u003etime * rec-\u003etime overflow will likely happen earlier."
              },
