            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-22016",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: dpll: fix xa_alloc_cyclic() error handling"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndpll: fix xa_alloc_cyclic() error handling\n\nIn case of returning 1 from xa_alloc_cyclic() (wrapping) ERR_PTR(1) will\nbe returned, which will cause IS_ERR() to be false. Which can lead to\ndereference not allocated pointer (pin).\n\nFix it by checking if err is lower than zero.\n\nThis wasn't found in real usecase, only noticed. Credit to Pierre."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-22016",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-22016\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-84.84\nLink: [CVE-2025-22016](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-22016)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndpll: fix xa_alloc_cyclic() error handling\n\nIn case of returning 1 from xa_alloc_cyclic() (wrapping) ERR_PTR(1) will\nbe returned, which will cause IS_ERR() to be false. Which can lead to\ndereference not allocated pointer (pin).\n\nFix it by checking if err is lower than zero.\n\nThis wasn't found in real usecase, only noticed. Credit to Pierre.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-22016**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-84.84|[CVE-2025-22016](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-22016)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndpll: fix xa_alloc_cyclic() error handling\n\nIn case of returning 1 from xa_alloc_cyclic() (wrapping) ERR_PTR(1) will\nbe returned, which will cause IS_ERR() to be false. Which can lead to\ndereference not allocated pointer (pin).\n\nFix it by checking if err is lower than zero.\n\nThis wasn't found in real usecase, only noticed. Credit to Pierre."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
