            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-37958",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: mm/huge_memory: fix dereferencing invalid pmd migration entry"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/huge_memory: fix dereferencing invalid pmd migration entry\n\nWhen migrating a THP, concurrent access to the PMD migration entry during\na deferred split scan can lead to an invalid address access, as\nillustrated below.  To prevent this invalid access, it is necessary to\ncheck the PMD migration entry and return early.  In this context, there is\nno need to use pmd_to_swp_entry and pfn_swap_entry_to_page to verify the\nequality of the target folio.  Since the PMD migration entry is locked, it\ncannot be served as the target.\n\nMailing list discussion and explanation from Hugh Dickins: \"An anon_vma\nlookup points to a location which may contain the folio of interest, but\nmight instead contain another folio: and weeding out those other folios is\nprecisely what the \"folio != pmd_folio((*pmd)\" check (and the \"risk of\nreplacing the wrong folio\" comment a few lines above it) is for.\"\n\nBUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ffffea60001db008\nCPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 2199114 Comm: tee Not tainted 6.14.0+ #4 NONE\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS 1.16.3-debian-1.16.3-2 04/01/2014\nRIP: 0010:split_huge_pmd_locked+0x3b5/0x2b60\nCall Trace:\n\u003cTASK\u003e\ntry_to_migrate_one+0x28c/0x3730\nrmap_walk_anon+0x4f6/0x770\nunmap_folio+0x196/0x1f0\nsplit_huge_page_to_list_to_order+0x9f6/0x1560\ndeferred_split_scan+0xac5/0x12a0\nshrinker_debugfs_scan_write+0x376/0x470\nfull_proxy_write+0x15c/0x220\nvfs_write+0x2fc/0xcb0\nksys_write+0x146/0x250\ndo_syscall_64+0x6a/0x120\nentry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x76/0x7e\n\nThe bug is found by syzkaller on an internal kernel, then confirmed on\nupstream."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37958",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-37958\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-90.91\nLink: [CVE-2025-37958](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37958)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/huge_memory: fix dereferencing invalid pmd migration entry\n\nWhen migrating a THP, concurrent access to the PMD migration entry during\na deferred split scan can lead to an invalid address access, as\nillustrated below.  To prevent this invalid access, it is necessary to\ncheck the PMD migration entry and return early.  In this context, there is\nno need to use pmd_to_swp_entry and pfn_swap_entry_to_page to verify the\nequality of the target folio.  Since the PMD migration entry is locked, it\ncannot be served as the target.\n\nMailing list discussion and explanation from Hugh Dickins: \"An anon_vma\nlookup points to a location which may contain the folio of interest, but\nmight instead contain another folio: and weeding out those other folios is\nprecisely what the \"folio != pmd_folio((*pmd)\" check (and the \"risk of\nreplacing the wrong folio\" comment a few lines above it) is for.\"\n\nBUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ffffea60001db008\nCPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 2199114 Comm: tee Not tainted 6.14.0+ #4 NONE\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS 1.16.3-debian-1.16.3-2 04/01/2014\nRIP: 0010:split_huge_pmd_locked+0x3b5/0x2b60\nCall Trace:\n\u003cTASK\u003e\ntry_to_migrate_one+0x28c/0x3730\nrmap_walk_anon+0x4f6/0x770\nunmap_folio+0x196/0x1f0\nsplit_huge_page_to_list_to_order+0x9f6/0x1560\ndeferred_split_scan+0xac5/0x12a0\nshrinker_debugfs_scan_write+0x376/0x470\nfull_proxy_write+0x15c/0x220\nvfs_write+0x2fc/0xcb0\nksys_write+0x146/0x250\ndo_syscall_64+0x6a/0x120\nentry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x76/0x7e\n\nThe bug is found by syzkaller on an internal kernel, then confirmed on\nupstream.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-37958**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-90.91|[CVE-2025-37958](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37958)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/huge_memory: fix dereferencing invalid pmd migration entry\n\nWhen migrating a THP, concurrent access to the PMD migration entry during\na deferred split scan can lead to an invalid address access, as\nillustrated below.  To prevent this invalid access, it is necessary to\ncheck the PMD migration entry and return early.  In this context, there is\nno need to use pmd_to_swp_entry and pfn_swap_entry_to_page to verify the\nequality of the target folio.  Since the PMD migration entry is locked, it\ncannot be served as the target.\n\nMailing list discussion and explanation from Hugh Dickins: \"An anon_vma\nlookup points to a location which may contain the folio of interest, but\nmight instead contain another folio: and weeding out those other folios is\nprecisely what the \"folio != pmd_folio((*pmd)\" check (and the \"risk of\nreplacing the wrong folio\" comment a few lines above it) is for.\"\n\nBUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ffffea60001db008\nCPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 2199114 Comm: tee Not tainted 6.14.0+ #4 NONE\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS 1.16.3-debian-1.16.3-2 04/01/2014\nRIP: 0010:split_huge_pmd_locked+0x3b5/0x2b60\nCall Trace:\n\u003cTASK\u003e\ntry_to_migrate_one+0x28c/0x3730\nrmap_walk_anon+0x4f6/0x770\nunmap_folio+0x196/0x1f0\nsplit_huge_page_to_list_to_order+0x9f6/0x1560\ndeferred_split_scan+0xac5/0x12a0\nshrinker_debugfs_scan_write+0x376/0x470\nfull_proxy_write+0x15c/0x220\nvfs_write+0x2fc/0xcb0\nksys_write+0x146/0x250\ndo_syscall_64+0x6a/0x120\nentry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x76/0x7e\n\nThe bug is found by syzkaller on an internal kernel, then confirmed on\nupstream."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-37974",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: s390/pci: Fix missing check for zpci_create_device() error return"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/pci: Fix missing check for zpci_create_device() error return\n\nThe zpci_create_device() function returns an error pointer that needs to\nbe checked before dereferencing it as a struct zpci_dev pointer. Add the\nmissing check in __clp_add() where it was missed when adding the\nscan_list in the fixed commit. Simply not adding the device to the scan\nlist results in the previous behavior."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37974",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-37974\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.8.0-64.67\nLink: [CVE-2025-37974](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37974)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/pci: Fix missing check for zpci_create_device() error return\n\nThe zpci_create_device() function returns an error pointer that needs to\nbe checked before dereferencing it as a struct zpci_dev pointer. Add the\nmissing check in __clp_add() where it was missed when adding the\nscan_list in the fixed commit. Simply not adding the device to the scan\nlist results in the previous behavior.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-37974**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.8.0-64.67|[CVE-2025-37974](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-37974)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/pci: Fix missing check for zpci_create_device() error return\n\nThe zpci_create_device() function returns an error pointer that needs to\nbe checked before dereferencing it as a struct zpci_dev pointer. Add the\nmissing check in __clp_add() where it was missed when adding the\nscan_list in the fixed commit. Simply not adding the device to the scan\nlist results in the previous behavior."
              },
              "properties": {
